WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.950
LIGHTMAN: Welcome.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.530
It's very nice to see
so many people here,

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:23.718
which I think is a tribute
to our speaker tonight.

00:00:23.718 --> 00:00:25.110
I'm Alan Lightman.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:30.690
And I'm head of MIT's Program in
Writing and Humanistic Studies,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.685
which is part of the School of
Humanities and Social Sciences.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.780
For many years now,
our program has

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:44.960
been sponsoring a writers series
in which we invite leading

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.590
thinkers and writers
to the MIT campus

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.940
to talk and to read
from their works.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:59.850
These events are all free
and open to the public.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.790
Tonight we are
honored to have Susan

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:08.970
Sontag as the first speaker
in this year's writer series.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.330
And I want to thank everyone
who has been involved

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.115
with planning tonight's events.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.450
I'm already dry.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:32.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.420
Susan Sontag is a
national treasure.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:43.020
Her intellectual and literary
talents span every genre.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.410
Her essays on American
culture, for example,

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:52.350
are characterized by insight and
beautiful writing and a deeply

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.910
philosophical perspective.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:02.160
Ms. Sontag's books include
the novels, The Benefactor,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.140
Death Kit, and her
recent, The Volcano Lover.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.350
Her essays have been
collected in such books

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.040
as Against
Interpretation, Styles

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:20.700
of Radical Will, On Photography,
and Under the Sign of Saturn.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.520
And she has two book length
essays, Illness as Metaphor,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.210
and AIDS and Its Metaphors.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:34.770
She has written and directed
four feature length films.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:39.150
And her new play Alice in Bed
has been produced in Germany

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:44.440
and receive its American
premiere next fall.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.390
She has won numerous awards.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:49.180
And I'll just mention a few.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.480
Her book, On Photography,
won the National Book Critics

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.860
Circle Award.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.250
From 1987 to 1989,
she was president

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.500
of the American
Center of PEN, which

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.120
is the international
writers organization.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:09.850
And in 1990, she was awarded
a very prestigious MacArthur

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.720
Fellowship.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.360
As many of you know, she
went to Sarajevo in July

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.140
to stage a production
of Waiting for Godot.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.860
That production has been
met with much acclaim.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.650
She wrote a beautiful
article describing it

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.620
in a recent issue of the
New York Review of Books.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:37.770
And she has been named an
honorary citizen of Sarajevo.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.410
We are honored to have Susan
Sontag with us tonight.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.390
In a few minutes, we
are going to be joined

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:51.590
by another honored guest.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.680
And for security reasons,
I ask that none of you

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.330
leave the room once the
program is underway.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.990
The event should last
until about 7:30.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:09.260
I'm sorry-- well, you
can see that I'm very

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.360
calm about this whole thing.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.795
The event should last
until about 9:30.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.740
And if any of you think that
you cannot remain in your seats

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.360
until 9:30, then I'm going to
give you the chance to leave

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.650
the room right now.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:30.860
So I'll wait.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.230
I'll wait just a minute.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.470
I want to give you
a chance to leave.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:42.999
I would recommend staying.

00:04:42.999 --> 00:04:45.494
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:17.166
OK, and I'm very, very happy to
introduce to you Susan Sontag.

00:05:17.166 --> 00:05:20.142
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:50.005
SONTAG: I want to begin this
evening by doing two things.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:06:00.700
First of all, I want to
speak about a friend of mine,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.040
the great English writer--

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.370
or perhaps I should
say British writer--

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:12.760
Salman Rushdie, who has
been under sentence of death

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:16.990
for almost five years.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:24.640
As many of you know,
on February 14, 1989

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.050
a fatwa or death
sentence was issued

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:34.040
by the Ayatollah
Khomeini of Iran,

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:41.530
which calls on Muslims
everywhere to kill Salman

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:48.520
Rushdie and also to kill his
publishers, his translators,

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.830
the booksellers, his
agents, presumably,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:57.470
by extension, the people
who buy and read his book.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:04.860
There is a bounty offered of
more than two million dollars

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:07.460
for the assassins.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.630
And there are
assassins everywhere

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:16.910
seeking Salman Rushdie,
hoping to collect this bounty.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:21.900
Why was Salman Rushdie
sentenced to death?

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.190
Why was a British citizen,
an English citizen

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:28.440
sentenced to death
by a religious leader

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.570
in a foreign country?

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.362
Because he wrote a novel.

00:07:34.362 --> 00:07:35.070
He wrote a novel.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.409
It wasn't even his first novel.

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:37.200
It's a great novel.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.730
But he'd written some other
great novels before it, notably

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.040
Midnight's Children.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.780
And between Midnight's Children
and the novel for which he

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:55.060
was sentenced to death,
a novel called Shame,

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:58.670
Salman Rushdie was already a
well-known and very admired

00:07:58.670 --> 00:07:59.170
writer.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:02.710
He is one of the great
writers of our time.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:10.060
Now he is famous and exemplary
for reasons that could hardly

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.040
be imagined a few years ago.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:14.860
And we've all had
a great education

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:19.120
thinking about the
predicament of Salman Rushdie.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.890
This has been going on, as I
say, for almost five years.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:29.710
On February 14, 1994,
it will be five years

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:35.409
that Salman Rushdie has lived
in hiding, under constant guard.

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:39.190
There are assassin teams
everywhere looking for him.

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:41.020
He has had many narrow escapes.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.850
He is obliged to move
from house to house.

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:46.600
This is the life he has
been living for five years.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.750
Assassins have sought,
and in a number

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:55.780
of cases, reached other
people his Japanese translator

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:59.980
was murdered in July, 1991.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.960
And his Italian translator,
on practically the same day--

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.140
it was a coordinated
assassination attempt--

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:12.280
was severely wounded
and very only luckily

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:15.670
and barely escaped
with his life.

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:19.000
And in October, just
a few weeks ago,

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.480
his Norwegian publisher,
an assassination attempt,

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:27.720
also nearly successful, was
made on his Norwegian publisher.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.540
It's worth mentioning
that none of the people,

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.100
of course, who are seeking
Salman Rushdie's life

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.120
to collect this bounty
have read this book,

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:43.680
have even seen a
copy of this book.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.660
And it's certainly
worth underscoring

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:50.940
that they do not represent
the great traditions of Islam.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:53.550
Just recently in
Paris, and I'm sure it

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:58.230
will be shortly translated
into English, 100 prominent--

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.320
indeed some of them
very great intellectuals

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.220
and writers from
the Islamic world--

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:11.470
declared their solidarity
with Salman Rushdie.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:15.220
And these include, for instance,
the great Egyptian novelist

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:18.410
Mahfouz who won the Nobel
Prize several years ago.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:28.270
The Rushdie case raises
the most urgent issues,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:31.510
issues that concern all of us.

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:34.030
The book, The
Satanic Verses, which

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:37.210
has been read by none of the
people who attack it and want

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:40.090
to murder Salman
Rushdie for writing it,

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:43.780
the book and the reactions
to it are very complex

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:46.720
and richly emblematic.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.600
But the man, the
writer, the human being,

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:53.560
and his predicament,
his constant danger

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.510
is not emblematic at all.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:58.990
It's absolutely real.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:03.670
Salman Rushdie is particularly
exemplary in the way

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.410
he has assumed his destiny.

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:08.890
Because in these very,
very painful and dangerous

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:13.570
and terrifying five years,
he's gone on being a writer.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.790
He's produced another novel.

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:18.367
He is working on--

00:11:18.367 --> 00:11:19.700
I'm sure I'm omitting something.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:23.210
He's been so active in the
midst of all this horror.

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:26.330
He is writing still
another novel.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:30.170
He has published a number
of extraordinary essays

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:34.970
and given speeches defending
the basic human right

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.880
to liberty of expression.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.945
He reviews books regularly.

00:11:39.945 --> 00:11:42.320
And some of you may have seen
his reviews in the New York

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:43.160
Times and elsewhere.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:48.360
He even gives blurbs to
writers, far too many, I think.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.300
But he is--

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.670
I can understand that he wants
to go on participating fully

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:56.810
in the literary world.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:02.950
What Salman Rushdie stands for
is the right to secularism,

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:07.920
pluralism, to freedom of
expression, to tolerance,

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:13.210
values that I hope we are all
united in supporting, values, I

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:18.780
might add, for which the
new nation-state of Bosnia

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.090
is also being murdered
for standing up

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:27.480
for values of pluralism and
secularism and tolerance.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:32.670
Salman Rushdie is only the
most visible individual victim

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:36.720
of a worldwide struggle
against tolerance,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:44.470
against anti-tribal
enlightened spirit of freedom.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:46.150
And he would be the
first to say that.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:50.230
He would be the first
to say my case stands

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:53.690
for many other cases as well.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:58.400
I think that his cause
honoring his rights

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:01.820
and his exemplary
conduct since the fatwa

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:10.150
should be our cause as, indeed,
while he lives minute by minute

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:14.020
under constant threat of
death, which has not abated,

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.810
he never forgets that
he is fighting not just

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:24.070
to stay alive, but to hold back
the immense force of terror,

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:31.560
those who would silence
honesty and strangle dialogue.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:35.070
Salman Rushdie is still alive.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.130
But his predicament,
the sentence

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:43.650
of terror and intimidation,
that has been carried out.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:47.970
And the worst kind of
censorship is self-censorship.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:52.080
This case is the
great test of where

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:56.220
we stand on the issue of
freedom and solidarity

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:58.680
and the future of
our own culture.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:07.050
That's the first thing I wanted
to do to open this evening.

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:10.250
And the second thing,
not surprisingly,

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:12.826
is I wanted to introduce
Salman Rushdie.

00:14:12.826 --> 00:14:15.306
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:05.060
Good evening.

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:06.160
My name is Mark Wrighton.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.530
I'm the provost of MIT.

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.960
And first, I want to
thank Susan Sontag

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:15.340
for joining us this evening, for
introducing our special guest,

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:17.470
and for making this
evening possible.

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:19.491
Would you join me
in thanking her?

00:15:19.491 --> 00:15:22.928
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.590
Unfortunately,
President Vest could not

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.950
be with us this evening,
owing to a prior commitment

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:42.560
outside Cambridge.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:45.630
But I am pleased to
present on his behalf

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:49.410
and on behalf of the MIT
community, a citation

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.410
and an award to Mr. Rushdie.

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:54.960
I will present the letter
that President Vest

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.810
has written to Mr. Rushdie.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.450
And in it he says,
"In recognition

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:02.070
of your outstanding
contributions

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.410
to world literature
and in recognition

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.960
of the independence of
thought and expression

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.170
that you have come to represent,
the Massachusetts Institute

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:14.370
of Technology proudly appoints
you honorary visiting professor

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:15.240
of the humanities."

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.222
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:39.100
It's a real privilege for me to
be on this stage this evening.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.340
So many people have worked
hard to make it possible,

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:45.060
especially Alan Lightman, the
head of our Program in Writing

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:48.510
and Humanistic Studies,
and Anita Desai, a person

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:52.020
we've been very fortunate
to attract to our faculty.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.910
This is a special
evening, of course.

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:57.510
We're very privileged to host
this distinguished speaker.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:16:58.560
We honor him.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:02.703
And we celebrate our
freedom of expression.

00:17:02.703 --> 00:17:06.140
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:10.259 --> 00:17:11.050
RUSHDIE: Thank you.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:14.990
Well I have to--

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:17.450
I have a lot of people
to thank, first of all.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.195
Good evening all of you.

00:17:19.195 --> 00:17:20.069
It's nice to be here.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:23.510
Although I was going to
apologize for the kind of cloak

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:25.910
and dagger aspect of this.

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:29.030
But actually Susan Sontag and
I have been looking forward

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:32.300
to this surprise for so long,
it's actually quite nice

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:33.670
to pull it off .

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:36.650
And first of all,
I want to thank her

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:39.230
for being what she
described as my beard.

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.390
It means I now have two.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:53.540
I also, of course,
would like to thank

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:57.920
MIT for this incredible honor,
which is, I have to say,

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:00.860
the first academic honor
I've ever received.

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:02.350
So it's nice to
start at the top.

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:11.290
I was saying just before we
all came on here that I was--

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:14.670
see, the plan for me is I was
supposed to be a scientist.

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:18.070
When I was 16,
there's no question

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:20.950
that my best subjects
were math and physics.

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:22.780
And everybody from
my parents downwards

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:24.610
assumed that that's
what I would do.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:28.540
But at some point, for reasons
of stubbornness and perversity,

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:32.500
I took a dramatic left turn and
did history, French, and Latin

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:33.700
instead.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:36.040
And I've always
regretted the loss

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:37.570
of that scientific dimension.

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:39.430
It's nice to kind
of get it back free.

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:50.350
And I really do hope that this
isn't just a symbolic moment,

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:53.680
but that it can be the beginning
of a long term relationship

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:57.110
with this great institution.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:00.610
I think many of us,
writers of my generation,

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:08.110
have felt that in many ways
the cutting edge of the new

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.130
is now to be found
in the sciences,

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:11.620
not in philosophy,
not in the arts,

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:14.080
and that actually
we have a great deal

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:19.310
to absorb and learn from.

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:23.050
And so I look forward
to being able to do that

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:27.310
in the company of all of you
and of the remarkable talents

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:32.429
and minds present at MIT.

00:19:32.429 --> 00:19:33.970
And I think for that
reason, the fact

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:37.750
that this is such a great
institution dedicated

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:42.820
to the search for the new and
to the openness of thought

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.430
that that search
requires, I think

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:50.080
it's very appropriate and very,
a very great privilege for me

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:55.340
to receive this honor from, of
all places, this institution.

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:59.220
And I thank them
once again for that.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:03.810
Susan Sontag spoke so eloquently
about the issues surrounding

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:07.590
the so-called Rushdie
case that there is not

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.410
very much I want to
add except to endorse

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:13.230
all the arguments she made.

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:16.080
I just wanted to say
one thing that it's

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:19.470
been going on for five years
and that's a very long time.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.000
It's also long
enough to understand

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.130
exactly what is going on.

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:26.310
For a long time, I
think, when this began,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:29.580
the fact that the
language of censorship,

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:33.330
of political oppression
and persecution

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.870
was wrapped in a kind
of cloak of religion

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.320
meant that people did
not see it plainly.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:43.710
And that's natural, in
a way, because that's

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:46.710
a language which people respect,
that if that language is used

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.560
to cloak evil, it
takes some time

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:53.100
for the evil inside the cloak
to make its presence felt.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:56.870
I think it's now perfectly
clear the nature of, the nature

00:20:56.870 --> 00:21:00.020
of the evil that lies
behind such threats

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:03.070
as the one aimed against
the Satanic Verses.

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.510
And I think it's very important
what Susan pointed out,

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:08.520
that this is not just a
threat against one individual.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:11.240
It's a threat against,
if you put together

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.420
all the people involved in
the production of a book,

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:16.970
literally thousands of
people in many countries.

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:19.400
And in fact, you could say
that I am the best protected

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.020
person of all those people.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:27.800
The shooting of William
Nygaard, my very courageous

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:32.124
Norwegian publisher makes
me feel determined or even

00:21:32.124 --> 00:21:34.040
more determined to try
and make sure that this

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:37.250
is the last such atrocity.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:40.160
And for that, I'm
afraid, I need your help.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:45.810
Because the only weapon that
I have is public opinion.

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:49.890
In the end, the governments
of the democratic world

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:52.770
will act in this
matter only if they

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:54.750
believe it's a matter
of sufficient interest

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:57.090
to their own
citizens and voters.

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:58.770
They won't do it for me.

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:01.480
They might do it for you.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:03.600
It's therefore
extremely important

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:07.630
that the citizens of
democratic countries

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:10.930
understand and make
plain that the case

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.870
of the murder of a
writer or a publisher

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:20.560
or a bookseller or a translator
is an insult to their freedom.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:23.840
It's not just an act
against the individual.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:27.070
The attempt to suppress the
book is the attempt not just

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:28.980
to suppress the right
of a writer to writer

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.840
or a publisher to publish or
a translator to translate,

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:34.840
it's also the attempt to
suppress the right of a reader

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:38.800
to read and of out of
all of us to choose

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.320
that which we choose to read.

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:45.490
So I ask you to continue to put
pressure on your own government

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:48.370
in order to make sure that
it as fully as possible

00:22:48.370 --> 00:22:52.060
joins the international
campaign against this threat.

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.980
For one final reason,
which is that I've

00:22:56.980 --> 00:23:03.800
sat down for five years,
that any form of terrorism

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:08.030
which seems to succeed
will be repeated.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:10.290
Here is the invention,
you might say,

00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:15.560
of a new form of terrorism,
a successor to two airplane

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:17.480
hijacks and the taking
of hostages here.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:20.030
We have a kind of
terrorism at a distance,

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:22.880
a kind of remote control
terrorism, which simply

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:27.200
points a finger across
the world and asks

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:29.630
for the elimination
of certain individuals

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:31.360
and offers financial rewards--

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:34.880
and not just financial
rewards, but the promise,

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:36.620
from a dubious
promise of paradise.

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:43.440
If this form of terrorism
seems to be working,

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:45.630
it will be repeated.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:49.440
And the sad fact is that it
is already being repeated.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.770
Within the last
few months, we've

00:23:52.770 --> 00:24:00.030
seen the assassination of the
writer Tahar Djaout of Algeria,

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:03.210
the assassination of
the secularists thinker,

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:09.650
Farag Foda in Egypt, the
offer of six figure sums

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:13.850
in dollars for the
assassination of the writer Aziz

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:18.270
Nesin in Turkey, and
most recently, the offer

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:22.680
of a large sum of money by
Islamic fanatics in Bangladesh

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:26.400
for the murder of the young
feminist novelist, Taslima

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:30.990
Nasrin whose crime,
whose so-called blasphemy

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:36.300
was that she wrote a novel
deploring the revenge

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:40.530
killings in Bangladesh of
Hindus by Islamic fanatics

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:45.510
after the destruction of the
Muslim-- the mosque in Ayodhya

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:46.860
in India.

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:50.340
For this, she is to
be sentenced to death.

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.190
Here is evidence, if
ever it was needed,

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:56.340
that this is a technique which
is beginning to look popular.

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:58.590
And the reason that's the
reason it's becoming popular

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:00.295
is that it seems to have worked.

00:25:00.295 --> 00:25:01.920
It's therefore
important to demonstrate

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:03.880
that it didn't work.

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:08.190
And I think in the end, the only
way you can show a terrorist

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:11.220
and what he is doing
doesn't work, is to say,

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:13.350
"I'm not scared of you."

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.810
The purpose of terrorism
is to terrorize.

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:17.580
The only defense
against terrorism

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:21.930
is to refuse to be terrorized.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:26.940
We have to, therefore,
avoid our own fear.

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:30.430
Because that can do the
terrorists' work for them.

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:32.850
And so it seemed to me
that instead of banging on

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:35.100
for one evening, just this
evening, instead of talking

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:37.391
about the government of Iran,
a subject in which I have

00:25:37.391 --> 00:25:42.840
incredibly little interest,
and instead of accepting

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:47.790
their decision to turn me into
some kind of demonized figure,

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:51.990
I would say I'm not that kind of
writer, I'm this kind of writer

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:53.630
and read to you.

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.550
And I thought I would read
not from The Satanic Verses,

00:25:56.550 --> 00:26:02.422
just for a change give
The Satanic Verses a rest,

00:26:02.422 --> 00:26:04.380
thought I'd read from an
earlier novel of mine,

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:11.560
Midnight's Children, a novel
which begins like this.

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:20.500
"I was born in the city of
Bombay, once upon a time.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:21.550
No, that won't do.

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:25.170
There's no getting
away from the date.

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:27.750
I was born in Doctor
Narlikar's Nursing Home

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:31.160
on August 15, 1947.

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:33.890
And the time, the
time matters too.

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:35.280
Well, then at night.

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:37.250
No, it's important to be more--

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:40.790
on the stroke of midnight,
as a matter of fact.

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:45.670
Clock-hands joined palms and
respectful greeting as I came.

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:47.260
Oh, spell it out, spell it out.

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:49.510
At the precise instant
of India's arrival

00:26:49.510 --> 00:26:53.200
at independence, I tumbled
forth into the world.

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.000
There were gasps.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:58.510
And, outside the window,
fireworks and crowds.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:02.590
A few seconds later, my
father broke his big toe.

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:04.570
But his accident
was a mere trifle

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:07.540
when set beside what
had befallen me.

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:12.190
Because thanks to the occult
tyrannies of those blindly

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:16.000
saluting clocks, I had been
mysteriously handcuffed

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.470
to history.

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:21.630
My destiny is indissolubly
chained to those of my country.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:25.110
For the next three decades,
there was to be no escape.

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:27.120
Soothsayers had prophesied me.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.430
Newspapers celebrated
my arrival.

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:32.910
Politicos ratified
my authenticity.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:36.170
I was left entirely without
a say in the matter.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:42.280
I, Saleem Sinai, later variously
called Snotnose, Stainface,

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:47.850
Baldy, Sniffer, Buddha,
and even Piece-of-the-Moon,

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:51.900
had become heavily embroiled
in fate, at the best of times,

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.490
a dangerous sort of involvement.

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:59.290
And I couldn't even wipe
my own nose at the time."

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:01.952
Well, that's how it begins.

00:28:01.952 --> 00:28:03.910
I'm just going to read
you a couple of passages

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:06.820
from later in the book.

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:11.920
They both happen about 10
years after that birth.

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:18.040
Saleem, by this
stage, what we know

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:20.450
about him is that he's not
really his parents' child

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:22.300
because he'd been
changed, changed in the--

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:26.110
swapped in the
hospital by accident.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.480
He's also, by this
stage, acquired

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:33.210
a kind of incipient
gift of telepathy,

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:37.060
which features somewhat
in what follows.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:40.710
His mother, or the
woman who he thinks

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.390
is his mother has
been previously

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:47.040
married, but separated from her
previous husband, is remarried.

00:28:47.040 --> 00:28:49.680
He has recently, owing to
his telepathic abilities,

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:54.000
also his habit of eavesdropping
on telephone calls,

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:59.960
discovered that she has begun
to have mysterious telephone

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:02.030
conversations with
a strange man.

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:10.530
So he decides to follow her
on one of these assignations

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:15.030
by hiding in the
trunk of her car.

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:18.180
"Once upon a time,
there was a mother,

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:21.990
who in order to become a mother,
had agreed to change her name,

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.550
who set herself
the task of falling

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:26.170
in love with her
husband bit-by-bit,

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:29.400
but who could never manage
to love one part, the part,

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:33.390
curiously enough, which made
possible her motherhood, whose

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.820
feet were hobbled by verrucas
and whose shoulders were

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:39.630
stooped beneath the accumulating
guilts of the world, whose

00:29:39.630 --> 00:29:42.210
husband's unlovable
organ failed to recover

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:46.320
from the effects of a freeze,
and who, like her husband,

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:48.900
finally succumbed to the
mysteries of telephones,

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:51.030
spending long minutes
listening to the words

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:54.090
of wrong-number
telephone callers.

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:57.420
Shortly after my 10th
birthday, Amina Sinai

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:00.660
resumed her recent practice
of leaving suddenly and always

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:06.450
immediately after a wrong
number on urgent shopping trips.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:08.700
But now, hidden in
the boot of the Rover,

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.920
there traveled
with her a stowaway

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:13.680
who lay hidden and protected
by stolen cushions,

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:17.760
clutching a thin strip of
pink plastic in his hand.

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:21.550
O, the suffering one undergoes
in the name of righteousness!

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:23.070
The bruising and the bumps!

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:26.610
The breathing in of rubbery
boot-air through jolted teeth!

00:30:26.610 --> 00:30:28.500
And constantly, the
fear of discovery.

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:30.060
'Suppose she really
does go shopping?

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:34.620
Will the boot suddenly fly open?

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:37.350
Will live chickens be flung
in, feet tied together,

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:41.400
wings clipped, fluttery pecky
birds invading my hidey-hole?

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:42.210
Will she see me?

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:45.510
My god, I'll have to
be quiet for a week.'

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:47.820
My knees drawn in
beneath my chin,

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:51.240
which was protected against
bumps by an old faded cushion.

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:53.670
I voyaged into the
unknown in the vehicle

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:55.920
of maternal perfidy.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.580
My mother was a cautious driver.

00:30:57.580 --> 00:30:59.640
She went slowly and
turned corners with care.

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:02.820
But afterwards, I was
bruised black and blue

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:05.850
and my Mary Pereira
berated me soundly

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:07.275
for getting into fights.

00:31:07.275 --> 00:31:08.800
'Arré God, what a thing.

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:11.220
It's a wonder they didn't
smash you to pieces completely.

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:14.280
My God, what will you grow up
into you, you bad black man,

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:19.500
you haddi-phaelwan, you
skin-and-bone wrestler!'

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:21.840
To take my mind off
the jolting darkness,

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.990
I entered, with extreme caution,
that part of my mother's

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:27.380
mind which was in charge
of driving operations,

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:30.930
and as a result was able
to follow her route.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.690
And also to discern in my
mother's habitually tidy mind,

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:36.709
an alarming degree of disorder.

00:31:36.709 --> 00:31:38.250
I was already
beginning in those days

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:42.360
to classify people by their
degree of internal tidiness

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:45.090
and to discover that I preferred
the messier type, whose

00:31:45.090 --> 00:31:47.520
thoughts spilling
constantly into one another

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:50.070
so that anticipatory
images of food

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:52.770
interfered with the serious
business of earning a living

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:54.840
and sexual fantasies
were superimposed

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:58.620
upon their political
musings, bore a closer

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:02.070
relationship to my own pell-mell
tumble of a brain in which

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:04.260
everything ran into
everything else

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:05.940
and the white dot
of consciousness

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:09.700
jumped about like a wild flea
from one thing to the next.

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:12.990
Amina Sinai, whose
assiduous ordering instincts

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:16.720
had provided her with a brain
of almost abnormal neatness,

00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:21.100
was a curious recruit to
the ranks of confusion.

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:24.850
We headed north, past Breach
Candy Hospital Mahalaxmi

00:32:24.850 --> 00:32:27.990
Temple, north along
Hornby Vellard,

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:31.660
past Vallabhbhai Stadium
and Haji Ali's island tomb,

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:35.640
north of what had once
been the island of Bombay.

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:38.650
We were heading towards the
anonymous mass of tenements

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:42.260
and fishing villages and
textile plants and film studios

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:46.150
that the city became in these
northern zones, an area which

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:48.280
was, in those days,
utterly unknown to me.

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:50.410
I rapidly became
disoriented and was then

00:32:50.410 --> 00:32:54.160
obliged to admit to
myself that I was lost.

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.680
At last, down an
unprepossessing side street

00:32:56.680 --> 00:32:58.990
full of drainpipe-sleepers
and bicycle repair

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:01.930
shops and tattered men
and boys, we stopped.

00:33:01.930 --> 00:33:05.410
Clusters of children assailed
my mother as she descended.

00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:07.240
And she, who could
never shoo away a fly,

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:12.310
handed out small coins, thus
enlarging the crowd enormously.

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:14.500
Eventually, she
struggled away from them

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:15.850
and headed down the street.

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:18.790
There was a boy, pleading,
'Gib the car poliss, Begum?

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:20.860
Number one A-class
poliss, Begum?

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:22.630
I watch car till you come.

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:23.170
Begum?

00:33:23.170 --> 00:33:24.250
I very fine watchman.

00:33:24.250 --> 00:33:24.850
Ask anyone.'

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:27.019
In some panic, I listened
in for her reply.

00:33:27.019 --> 00:33:29.560
How could I get out of this boot
under the eyes of a guardian

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:30.670
urchin?

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:32.377
There was the
embarrassment of it.

00:33:32.377 --> 00:33:34.210
And besides, my emergence
would have created

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:36.500
a sensation in the street.

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:38.910
My mother said no.

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:40.620
She was disappearing
down the street.

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:43.590
The would-be polisher and
watchman gave up eventually.

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:45.570
There was a moment
when all eyes turned

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:47.550
to watch the passing
of a second car,

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:50.850
just in case it too stopped to
disgorge a lady who gave away

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:52.585
coins as if they were nuts.

00:33:52.585 --> 00:33:53.950
And in that instant--

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:55.950
I had been looking through
several pairs of eyes

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:57.940
to help me choose my moment--

00:33:57.940 --> 00:33:59.850
I performed my trick
with the pink plastic

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:04.170
and was out in the street beside
the closed car boot in a flash.

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:05.850
Setting my lips
grimly and ignoring

00:34:05.850 --> 00:34:08.489
all outstretched palms, I
set off in the direction

00:34:08.489 --> 00:34:11.070
of my mother had taken,
a pocket sized sleuth

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:14.520
with the nose of a bloodhound--"
he has a very big nose--

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:17.070
"and a loud drum pounding in
the place where my heart should

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:20.520
have been and arrived a few
minutes later at the Pioneer

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:22.500
Café.

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:27.090
Dirty glass in the window,
dirty glasses on the tables,

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:30.929
the Pioneer Café was not much
when compared to the Gaylords

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:33.500
and Kwalitys of the city's
more glamorous parts,

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:36.570
a real rutputty joint with
painted boards proclaiming

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:39.810
'Lovely Lassi' and 'Funtabulous
Falooda' and 'Bhel-Puribombay

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:43.500
Fashion,' with filmi playback
music blaring out from a cheap

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:47.460
radio by the cash-till a
long, narrow greeny room lit

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:51.030
by flickering neon, a forbidding
world in which broken-toothed

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:54.330
men sat at reccine-covered
tables with crumpled cards

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.340
and expressionless eyes.

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:58.800
But for all its
grimy decrepitude,

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:02.280
the Pioneer Café was a
repository of many dreams.

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:04.710
Early each morning, it would
be full of the best-looking

00:35:04.710 --> 00:35:08.280
ne'er-do-wells in the city, all
the goondas and taxi-drivers

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:11.100
and petty smugglers and
racecourse tipsters who had

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:14.460
once ago, once long ago arrived
in the city dreaming of film

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:18.600
stardom, of grotesquely vulgar
homes and black money payments.

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:21.360
Because every morning at six,
the major studios would send

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:26.280
minor functionaries to the
Pioneer Café to rope in extras

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:28.380
for the day's shooting.

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:31.320
For half an hour each
morning when DW Rama Studios

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:35.040
and Filmistan Talkies and RK
Films were taking their pick,

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:38.910
the Pioneer was the focus of all
the city's ambitions and hopes.

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:41.070
Then the studio scouts
left, accompanied

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:42.660
by the day's lucky ones.

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:47.450
And the Café emptied into its
habitual, neon-lit torpor.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:50.270
Around lunchtime, a different
set of dreams walked

00:35:50.270 --> 00:35:53.540
into the Café to spend the
afternoon hunched over cards

00:35:53.540 --> 00:35:56.240
and Lovely Lassi
and rough biris--

00:35:56.240 --> 00:35:58.520
different men with
different hopes.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:01.220
I didn't know it then,
but the afternoon Pioneer

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:04.500
was a notorious
Communist Party hangout.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:06.690
It was afternoon.

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:09.270
I saw my mother enter
the Pioneer Café.

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:12.000
Not daring to follow her,
I stayed in the street

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:14.400
pressing my nose against
a spider-webbed corner

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:16.530
of the grubby window pane.

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:18.930
Ignoring the curious
glances I got--

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:21.660
because my whites, although
boot-stained were nevertheless

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:24.540
starched, my hair, although
rumpled, was well oiled,

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:27.300
my shoes, scuffed as they
were, were still the plimsolls

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:28.950
of a prosperous child--

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:30.480
I followed her
with my eyes as she

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:34.290
went hesitantly and
verruca-hobbled past rickety

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:36.540
tables and hard-eyed men.

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:39.510
I saw my mother sit down at a
shadowed table at the far end

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:41.130
of the narrow cavern.

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:44.830
And then I saw the man
who rose to greet her.

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:46.640
The skin on his
face hung in folds

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:49.120
which revealed that he
had once been overweight.

00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:51.340
His teeth were
stained with paan.

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:53.680
He wore a clean white
kurta with Lucknow-work

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:55.300
around the buttonholes.

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:58.390
He had long hair,
poetically long,

00:36:58.390 --> 00:37:00.140
hanging lankly over his ears.

00:37:00.140 --> 00:37:03.340
But the top of his head
was bald and shiny.

00:37:03.340 --> 00:37:05.940
Forbidden syllables
echoed in my ears.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:06.655
Na.

00:37:06.655 --> 00:37:07.250
Dir.

00:37:07.250 --> 00:37:08.080
Nadir."

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:10.534
That's the name of
the first husband.

00:37:10.534 --> 00:37:12.700
"I realized that I wished
desperately that I'd never

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:15.590
resolved to come.

00:37:15.590 --> 00:37:18.830
Once upon a time, there
was an underground husband

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:22.010
who fled, leaving loving
messages of divorce, a poet

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:24.720
whose verses didn't even rhyme.

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:27.510
After a lost decade, he
emerged from goodness

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:30.090
knows where, his skin
hanging loose in memory

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:31.920
of his erstwhile plumpness.

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:34.140
And like his once
upon a time wife,

00:37:34.140 --> 00:37:36.120
he had acquired a new name.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:39.240
Nadir Khan was now Qasim
Khan, official candidate

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:41.640
of the official
Communist Party of India.

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:44.130
Lal Qasim, Qasim the Red.

00:37:44.130 --> 00:37:46.020
Nothing is without meaning.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:49.550
Not without reason
are blushes red.

00:37:49.550 --> 00:37:51.650
My uncle said, 'Watch
out for the Communists!'

00:37:51.650 --> 00:37:53.630
And my mother turned scarlet.

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:57.620
Politics and emotions
were united in her cheeks.

00:37:57.620 --> 00:38:00.410
Through the dirty, square,
glossy cinema-screen

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:03.560
of the Pioneer Café's
window, I watched Amina Sinai

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:07.100
and the no-longer-Nadir
play out their love scene.

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:11.570
They performed with the
ineptitude of genuine amateurs.

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:17.890
On the table, a packet of
cigarettes, State Express 555.

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:19.860
Numbers too have significance.

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:24.460
420, the name given to frauds,
1,001, the number of night,

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:26.380
of magic, of
alternative realities,

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:28.330
a number beloved of
poets and detested

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:31.390
by politicians for whom
all alternative versions

00:38:31.390 --> 00:38:35.260
of the world are threats,
and 555, which for years I

00:38:35.260 --> 00:38:37.120
believed to be the most
sinister of numbers,

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:41.840
the cipher of the Devil, the
Great Beast, Shaitan himself!

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:45.990
But the true demonic
number is not 555, but 666.

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:49.440
Yet in my mind, a dark aura
hangs around the three fives

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.240
to this day.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:53.360
But I am getting carried away.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:56.930
Suffice to say that Nadir
Qasim's preferred brand was

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:59.840
the aforesaid State Express,
that the figure five was

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:01.430
repeated three
times on the packet,

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:05.390
and then the manufacturers
were WD and HO Wells.

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:07.610
Unable to look into
my mother's face,

00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:10.160
I concentrated on
the cigarette packet,

00:39:10.160 --> 00:39:13.280
cutting from too short of
lovers to this extreme close-up

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:15.570
of nicotine.

00:39:15.570 --> 00:39:18.860
But now, hands into the frame.

00:39:18.860 --> 00:39:24.160
First, the hands of Nadir Qasim,
their poetic softness somewhat

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:28.870
calloused these days, hands
flickering like candle flames,

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:32.300
creeping forward across
reccine, then jerking back.

00:39:32.300 --> 00:39:36.170
Next, a woman's
hands, black as jet,

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:39.440
inching forward like
elegant spiders,

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:42.350
hands lifting up
off the table top,

00:39:42.350 --> 00:39:45.590
hands hovering
above three fives,

00:39:45.590 --> 00:39:47.810
beginning the
strangest of dances--

00:39:47.810 --> 00:39:51.890
rising, falling,
circling one another,

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:56.150
weaving in and out between each
other, hands longing for touch,

00:39:56.150 --> 00:40:00.020
hands outstretching,
tensing, quivering,

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:03.650
demanding to be, but always
at last jerking back.

00:40:03.650 --> 00:40:06.920
Fingertips avoiding fingertips
because what I'm watching here

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:09.690
on my dirty glass cinema
screen is, after all,

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:12.320
an Indian movie, in
which physical contact

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:14.750
is forbidden lest it
corrupt the watching

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:17.130
flower of Indian youth.

00:40:17.130 --> 00:40:20.520
And there are feet beneath
the table and faces above it,

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:22.950
feet advancing
towards feet, faces

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:26.610
tumbling softly towards
faces, but jerking away

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:30.700
all of a sudden in a
cruel censor's cut.

00:40:30.700 --> 00:40:33.610
Two strangers, each
bearing a screen name which

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.620
is not the name of
their birth, act out

00:40:35.620 --> 00:40:38.230
their half unwanted roles.

00:40:38.230 --> 00:40:42.070
I left the movie before
the end to slip back

00:40:42.070 --> 00:40:45.070
into the boot of the
unpolished, unwatched Rover,

00:40:45.070 --> 00:40:48.340
wishing I hadn't gone to see
it, unable to resist wanting

00:40:48.340 --> 00:40:51.540
to watch it all over again.

00:40:51.540 --> 00:40:55.080
What I saw at the
very end, my mother's

00:40:55.080 --> 00:41:00.150
hands raising a half empty glass
of Lovely Lassi, my mother's

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:02.910
lips pressing
gently, nostalgically

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:06.630
against the mottled
glass, my mother's hands

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.960
handing the glass
to her Nadir Qasim,

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:13.590
who also applied to the
opposite side of the glass

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:16.960
his own poetic mouth.

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:21.200
So it was that life imitated
bad art and my film director

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:22.600
sister--

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:26.020
my uncle, the film director's,
sister brought the eroticism

00:41:26.020 --> 00:41:30.280
of the indirect kiss into
the green neon dinginess

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:32.920
of the Pioneer Café.

00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:38.410
To sum up, in the high summer of
1957 at the peak of an election

00:41:38.410 --> 00:41:41.140
campaign, Amina Sinai
blushed inexplicably

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:44.740
at a chance mention of the
Communist Party of India.

00:41:44.740 --> 00:41:46.840
Her son followed her into
the north of the city

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:51.790
and spied on a pain-filled
scene of impotent love.

00:41:51.790 --> 00:41:56.890
Questions-- did I ever,
after that time employ

00:41:56.890 --> 00:42:00.150
the services of pink plastic?

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:04.650
Did I return to the cafe
of extras and Marxists?

00:42:04.650 --> 00:42:07.320
Did I confront my mother
with the heinous nature

00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:08.700
of her offense?

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:11.400
Because what mother has any
business to-- never mind about

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:14.310
what once-upon-a-time in
full view of her only son,

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:15.047
how could she?

00:42:15.047 --> 00:42:15.630
How could she?

00:42:15.630 --> 00:42:18.020
How could she?

00:42:18.020 --> 00:42:23.910
Answers-- I did not,
I did not, I did not."

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:31.520
Well that's-- a little later
something else not very

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:32.730
pleasant happens to him.

00:42:32.730 --> 00:42:33.950
He's having a bad 10th year--

00:42:37.377 --> 00:42:39.460
11th year, I beg his pardon,
he's already been 10.

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:48.420
"The first mutilation of
Saleem Sinai, which was rapidly

00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:52.230
followed by the second,
took place one Wednesday

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:57.270
early in 1958, the Wednesday
of the much-anticipated social

00:42:57.270 --> 00:43:00.090
under the auspices of the
Anglo-Scottish Education

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:01.380
Society.

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:03.280
That is to say, it
happened at school.

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:09.240
Saleem's assailant--
handsome, frenetic,

00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:11.640
with a barbarian's
shaggy mustache.

00:43:11.640 --> 00:43:14.250
I present the leaping,
hair-tearing figure

00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:18.630
of Mr. Emil Zagallo who taught
us geography and gymnastics

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:21.240
and who, that morning,
unintentionally precipitated

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:23.490
the crisis of my life.

00:43:23.490 --> 00:43:27.810
Zagallo claimed to be Peruvian
and was fond of calling us

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:30.570
jungle-Indians, bead lovers.

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.900
He hung a print of a stern,
sweaty soldier in a pointy tin

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:36.870
hat and metal pantaloons
above his blackboard

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:40.350
and had a way of stabbing a
finger at it in times of stress

00:43:40.350 --> 00:43:43.860
and shouting, 'You
see him, you savages?

00:43:43.860 --> 00:43:46.560
This man is civilization!

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:47.760
You show him respect.

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:49.830
He's got a sword!'

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:54.540
And he would swish his cane
through the stonewalled air.

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:58.500
We called him Pagal-Zagal,
crazy Zagallo.

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:01.800
Because for all his talk
of llamas and conquistadors

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:05.430
and the Pacific Ocean, we knew
with the absolute certainty

00:44:05.430 --> 00:44:09.270
of rumor that he'd been
born in a Mazagaon tenement

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:11.430
and his Goanese mother
had been abandoned

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:13.110
by a decamped shipping agent.

00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:17.880
So he was not only an Anglo,
but probably a bastard as well.

00:44:17.880 --> 00:44:20.670
Knowing this, we understood
why Zagallo affected

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:24.090
his Latin accent and also
why he was always in a fury,

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:27.090
why he beat his fists
against the stone wall, walls

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:28.390
of the classroom.

00:44:28.390 --> 00:44:31.170
But the knowledge didn't
stop us being afraid.

00:44:31.170 --> 00:44:34.500
And this Wednesday morning,
we knew we were in for trouble

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:36.860
because Optional Cathedral
had been canceled.

00:44:39.630 --> 00:44:41.250
The Wednesday
morning double period

00:44:41.250 --> 00:44:43.060
was Zagallo's geography class.

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:45.150
But only idiots and boys
with bigoted parents

00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:47.310
attended it because
it was also the time

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:50.430
when we could choose to troop
off to St. Thomas's Cathedral

00:44:50.430 --> 00:44:53.520
in crocodile formation,
a long line of boys

00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:56.730
of every conceivable religious
denomination escaping

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:59.190
from school into the
bosom of the Christians'

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:00.720
considerately optional god.

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:06.750
It drove Zagallo wild.

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.490
But he was helpless.

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:10.770
Today, however, there was
a dark glint in his eye

00:45:10.770 --> 00:45:12.750
because Mr. Crusoe,
the headmaster

00:45:12.750 --> 00:45:14.370
had announced at
morning assembly

00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:16.950
that Cathedral was canceled.

00:45:16.950 --> 00:45:19.200
In a bare, scraped
voice he sentenced

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:22.260
us, sentenced us to double
geography and Pagal-Zagal,

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:24.600
taking us all by
surprise because we

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:26.640
hadn't realized that
God was permitted

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:30.360
to exercise an option too.

00:45:30.360 --> 00:45:33.450
Glumly, we trooped
into Zagallo's lair.

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:35.070
One of the poor
idiots whose parents

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:36.720
never allowed them
to go to Cathedral

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:39.690
whispered viciously into
my ear, 'You just wait.

00:45:39.690 --> 00:45:42.960
He'll really get
you guys today.'

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:45.420
He really did.

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:47.850
Seated gloomily in class--

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:51.390
Glandy Keith Colaco,
Fat Perce Fishwala,

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:53.010
Jimmy Kapadia the
scholarship boy

00:45:53.010 --> 00:45:56.400
whose father was a taxi driver,
Hairoil Sabarmati, Sonny

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:58.950
Ibrahim, Cyrus-the-Great,
and I. Others, too,

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:00.430
but there's no time now.

00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:02.355
Because, with his eyes
narrowing in daylight,

00:46:02.355 --> 00:46:06.176
crazy Zagallo is
calling us to order.

00:46:06.176 --> 00:46:11.660
'Human geography,' Zagallo
announces, 'this is what,

00:46:11.660 --> 00:46:13.110
Kapadia?'

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:14.732
'Please sir, don't know, sir.'

00:46:14.732 --> 00:46:15.690
Hands fly into the air.

00:46:15.690 --> 00:46:19.140
Five belong to church banned
idiots, the sixth inevitably

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:20.460
to Cyrus-the-Great.

00:46:20.460 --> 00:46:22.910
But Zagallo is out
for blood today.

00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:25.050
The godly are going to suffer.

00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:28.360
'Filth from the jungle--'
he buffets Jimmy Kapadia,

00:46:28.360 --> 00:46:30.590
then begins to twist
an ear casually--

00:46:30.590 --> 00:46:33.810
'stay in class sometimes,
find out.' 'Ow ow ow yes, sir,

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:34.380
sorry, sir.'

00:46:34.380 --> 00:46:36.150
Six hands are waving,
but Jimmy's ear

00:46:36.150 --> 00:46:37.890
is in danger of cutting off.

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:40.980
Heroism gets the better of me.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:41.970
'Sir, please stop, sir.

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:46.830
He has a heart condition,
sir,' which is true,

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:48.210
but the truth is dangerous.

00:46:48.210 --> 00:46:52.800
Because now Zagallo is rounding
on me. 'So, a little arguer,

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:54.220
is it?'

00:46:54.220 --> 00:46:58.090
And I am being led by my hair
to the front of the class.

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:00.400
Under the relieved eyes
of my fellow pupils--

00:47:00.400 --> 00:47:02.730
thank god it's him, not us--

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:06.100
I writhe in agony
beneath imprisoned tufts.

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:08.020
'So answer the question.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:10.810
You know what is
human geography?'

00:47:10.810 --> 00:47:13.150
Pain fills my head,
obliterating all notions

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:14.930
of telepathic cheatery.

00:47:14.930 --> 00:47:17.650
'Aiee, sir, no sir, ouch.'

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:21.310
And now it is possible to
observe a joke descending

00:47:21.310 --> 00:47:24.340
on Zagallo, a joke
pulling his face

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:26.950
into the simulacrum of a smile.

00:47:26.950 --> 00:47:29.890
It is possible to watch
his hand darting forward,

00:47:29.890 --> 00:47:32.830
thumb and forefinger
extended, to note

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:36.790
how thumb and forefinger close
around the tip of my nose

00:47:36.790 --> 00:47:39.800
and pull downwards.

00:47:39.800 --> 00:47:43.560
Where the nose leads
the head must follow.

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:45.470
And finally, the
nose is hanging down

00:47:45.470 --> 00:47:47.840
and my eyes are
obliged to stare damply

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:50.960
at Zagallo's sandalled feet
with their dirty toenails

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:54.520
while Zagallo unleashes his wit.

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:57.640
'See, boys, you see
what we have here?

00:47:57.640 --> 00:48:01.180
Regard please the hideous face
of this primitive creature.

00:48:01.180 --> 00:48:04.280
It reminds you of--'
and the eager responses.

00:48:04.280 --> 00:48:05.770
'Sir, the devil,
sir.' 'Please sir,

00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:08.320
one cousin of mine.' 'No
sir, a vegetable, sir,

00:48:08.320 --> 00:48:10.930
I don't know which.'

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:14.320
Until Zagallo shouting
above the tumult, 'Silence!

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:16.910
Sons of baboons!

00:48:16.910 --> 00:48:20.260
This object here--'
a tug on my nose--

00:48:20.260 --> 00:48:22.632
'this is human geography.'

00:48:22.632 --> 00:48:23.590
'How sir?' 'Where sir?'

00:48:23.590 --> 00:48:25.000
What sir?'

00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:28.630
Zagallo is laughing now.
'You don't see,' he guffaws,

00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:32.110
'in the face of this ugly ape
you don't see the whole map

00:48:32.110 --> 00:48:33.430
of India?'

00:48:33.430 --> 00:48:36.630
'Yes sir.' 'No sir.'
'You show us sir.'

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:39.680
'See here, the Deccan
peninsula hanging down.'

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:42.290
Again, ouch, my nose.

00:48:42.290 --> 00:48:44.980
'Sir, sir, if that's the map
of India, what are the stains,

00:48:44.980 --> 00:48:46.740
sir?'

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:50.130
It is Glandy Keith
Colaco feeling bold.

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:52.610
Sniggers, titters
from my fellows,

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.840
and Zagallo taking the
question in his stride,

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:59.310
'These stains,' he
cries, 'are Pakistan!

00:48:59.310 --> 00:49:02.670
This birthmark on his right
here is the East Wing,

00:49:02.670 --> 00:49:05.860
this horrible stain
left cheek, the West!

00:49:05.860 --> 00:49:10.080
Remember, stupid boys,
Pakistan is a stain on the face

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:11.680
of India.'

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:16.490
'Ho ho' the class loves,
'absolute master joke, sir.'

00:49:16.490 --> 00:49:19.290
But now my nose has had enough.

00:49:19.290 --> 00:49:21.480
Staging its own
unprompted revolt

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:24.240
against the grasping
thumb and forefinger,

00:49:24.240 --> 00:49:28.830
it unleashes a
weapon of its own,

00:49:28.830 --> 00:49:33.930
a large blob of shining goo
emerges from the left nostril

00:49:33.930 --> 00:49:38.650
to plop into Mr. Zagallo's palm.

00:49:38.650 --> 00:49:43.590
Fat Perce Fishwala yells,
'Look at that, sir!

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:45.386
The drip from his nose, sir!

00:49:45.386 --> 00:49:47.320
Is that supposed to be Ceylon?'

00:49:47.320 --> 00:49:52.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:59.190
His palm smeared with goo,
Zagallo loses his joking mood.

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:03.360
'Animal,' he curses me,
'you see what you do?'

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:07.290
Zagallo's hand releases
my nose, returns to hair.

00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:11.390
Nasal refuse is wiped into
my neatly-parted locks.

00:50:11.390 --> 00:50:14.040
And now, once again,
my hair is seized.

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:17.150
Once again the hand is
pulling, but upwards now.

00:50:17.150 --> 00:50:18.570
And my head is jerked upright.

00:50:18.570 --> 00:50:20.380
My feet are moving on to tiptoe.

00:50:20.380 --> 00:50:22.100
And Zagallo, 'What are you?

00:50:22.100 --> 00:50:23.400
Tell me what you are.'

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.080
'Sir, an animal, sir.'

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:26.920
The hand pulls harder, higher.

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:30.060
'Again.' Standing on my
toenails now, I yelp, 'Aiee sir,

00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:32.940
an animal, an animal,
please sir, aiee!'

00:50:32.940 --> 00:50:35.790
And still harder, still
higher. 'Once more!'

00:50:35.790 --> 00:50:38.160
But suddenly it ends.

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:40.610
My feet are flat on
the ground again.

00:50:40.610 --> 00:50:45.020
And the class has fallen
into a deathly hush.

00:50:45.020 --> 00:50:50.900
'Sir, Sonny Ibrahim is saying,
'you pulled his hair out sir.'

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:53.107
And now the cacophony.
'Look sir, blood.'

00:50:53.107 --> 00:50:55.190
'He's bleeding, sir.'
'Please sir shall I take him

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:57.900
to the nurse?'

00:50:57.900 --> 00:51:01.510
Mr. Zagallo stood like
a statue with a lump

00:51:01.510 --> 00:51:06.550
of my hair in his fist while I,
too shocked to feel any pain,

00:51:06.550 --> 00:51:08.800
felt the patch on my head
where Mr. Zagallo had

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:12.580
created a monkish tonsure, a
circle where hair would never

00:51:12.580 --> 00:51:15.600
grow again.

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:18.390
Two days later, Croaker
Crusoe announced

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:21.390
that, unfortunately, Mr.
Emil Zagallo was leaving

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:23.850
the staff for personal reasons.

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:26.210
But I knew what
the reasons were.

00:51:26.210 --> 00:51:28.320
My uprooted hairs had
stuck to his hands

00:51:28.320 --> 00:51:30.070
like bloodstains that
wouldn't wash out.

00:51:30.070 --> 00:51:32.160
And nobody wants a teacher
with hair on his palms.

00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:35.250
'The first sign of madness,'
as Glandy Keith was fond

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:39.370
of saying, 'and the second
sign is looking for them.'

00:51:39.370 --> 00:51:43.130
Zagallo's legacy-- a
monk's tonsure, and worse

00:51:43.130 --> 00:51:45.590
than that, a whole
set of new taunts

00:51:45.590 --> 00:51:47.900
which my classmates
flung at me while we

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:50.000
waited for school buses
to take us home to get

00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:51.520
dressed for social.

00:51:51.520 --> 00:51:57.770
'Snotnose is a baldy' and
'Sniffer has a map face.'

00:51:57.770 --> 00:52:00.020
When Cyrus arrived
in the bus queue,

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:02.480
I tried to turn the crowd
against him by trying to set up

00:52:02.480 --> 00:52:05.000
a chant of 'Cyrus-the-Great,
born on a plate,

00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:09.820
in 1948,' but nobody
took up the offer.

00:52:09.820 --> 00:52:14.610
So we come to the events of
the Cathedral School Social

00:52:14.610 --> 00:52:17.410
at which bullies became
instruments of destiny

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:20.200
and fingers were
transmuted into fountains

00:52:20.200 --> 00:52:22.630
and Masha Miovic, the
legendary breast-stroker,

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:25.040
fell into a dead faint.

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:27.910
I arrived at the social with
the nurse's bandage still

00:52:27.910 --> 00:52:29.290
on my head.

00:52:29.290 --> 00:52:31.870
I was late because it hadn't
been easy to persuade my mother

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:33.490
to let me come.

00:52:33.490 --> 00:52:36.100
So by the time I stepped
into the assembly hall,

00:52:36.100 --> 00:52:39.040
beneath streamers and balloons
and the professionally

00:52:39.040 --> 00:52:42.850
suspicious gazes of
bony female chaperons,

00:52:42.850 --> 00:52:45.550
all the best girls were
already box-stepping and

00:52:45.550 --> 00:52:48.940
Mexican-Hatting with
absurdly smug partners.

00:52:48.940 --> 00:52:51.940
Naturally, the prefects
had the pick of the ladies.

00:52:51.940 --> 00:52:55.300
I watched them with passionate
envy, Guzder and Joshi

00:52:55.300 --> 00:52:58.030
and Stevenson and Rushdie--"
personal appearance--

00:52:58.030 --> 00:53:03.250
"Talyarkhan and Tayabali
and Jussawalla and Wagle.

00:53:03.250 --> 00:53:05.890
I tried butting in on them
during excuse-me's but when

00:53:05.890 --> 00:53:08.020
they saw my bandage
and my cucumber

00:53:08.020 --> 00:53:10.160
of a nose and the
stains on my face,

00:53:10.160 --> 00:53:12.460
they just laughed and
turned their backs.

00:53:12.460 --> 00:53:16.450
Hatred burgeoning in my bosom,
I ate potato chips and drank

00:53:16.450 --> 00:53:20.770
Bubble-Up and Vimto and
told myself, 'Those jerks,

00:53:20.770 --> 00:53:23.500
if they knew who I was, they'd
get out of my way pretty damn

00:53:23.500 --> 00:53:24.430
quick.'

00:53:24.430 --> 00:53:28.510
But still, the fear of revealing
my true telepathic nature

00:53:28.510 --> 00:53:31.270
was stronger than my
somewhat abstract desire

00:53:31.270 --> 00:53:35.210
for the whirling European girls.

00:53:35.210 --> 00:53:37.660
'Hey, Saleem, isn't it?

00:53:37.660 --> 00:53:40.070
Hey, man, what happened to you?'

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:43.900
I was dragged out of my
bitter solitary reverie

00:53:43.900 --> 00:53:46.780
by a voice behind
my left shoulder,

00:53:46.780 --> 00:53:52.670
a low, throaty voice, full
of promises, but also menace,

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:54.810
a girl's voice.

00:53:54.810 --> 00:53:57.810
I turned with a sort of
jump and found myself

00:53:57.810 --> 00:54:00.180
staring at a vision
with golden hair

00:54:00.180 --> 00:54:02.700
and a prominent
and famous chest.

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:04.240
My god, she was 14 years old.

00:54:04.240 --> 00:54:05.980
Why was she talking to me?

00:54:05.980 --> 00:54:06.480
'My

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:09.730
Name is Masha Miovic,'
the vision said,

00:54:09.730 --> 00:54:11.550
'I've met your sister.'

00:54:11.550 --> 00:54:14.160
Of course, of course,
'I know,' I stuttered,

00:54:14.160 --> 00:54:16.290
'I know your name.'

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:17.370
'And I know yours.'

00:54:17.370 --> 00:54:18.750
She straightened my tie.

00:54:18.750 --> 00:54:20.720
'So that's fair.'

00:54:20.720 --> 00:54:23.980
Over her shoulders, I saw Glandy
Keith and Fat Perce watching us

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:26.540
in drooling paroxysms of envy.

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:29.820
I straightened my back and
pushed out my shoulders.

00:54:29.820 --> 00:54:32.960
Masha Miovic asked
again about my bandage.

00:54:32.960 --> 00:54:37.650
'It's nothing,' I said in
what I hoped was a deep voice,

00:54:37.650 --> 00:54:41.540
'a sporting accident.'

00:54:41.540 --> 00:54:44.960
And then, working feverishly
to hold my voice steady,

00:54:44.960 --> 00:54:49.120
'Would you like to, to dance?'

00:54:49.120 --> 00:54:53.880
'OK,' said Masha Miovic, 'but
don't try any smooching.'

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:56.940
Saleem takes the floor
with Masha Miovic swearing

00:54:56.940 --> 00:54:58.620
not to smooch.

00:54:58.620 --> 00:55:00.990
Saleem and Masha are
doing the Mexican Hat.

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:03.730
Masha and Saleem box-stepping
with the best of them.

00:55:03.730 --> 00:55:07.980
I allow my face to adopt
a superior expression.

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:11.520
You see, you don't have to
be a prefect to get a girl.

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:12.990
The dance ended.

00:55:12.990 --> 00:55:16.260
And still, on top of by
wave of elation, I said,

00:55:16.260 --> 00:55:22.890
'Would you care for a stroll,
you know, in the quad?'

00:55:22.890 --> 00:55:25.490
Masha Miovic smiling privately.

00:55:25.490 --> 00:55:28.483
'Well, yeah, just for a
sec, but hands off, OK?'

00:55:28.483 --> 00:55:31.250
Hands off, Saleem swears.

00:55:31.250 --> 00:55:33.480
Saleem and Masha,
taking the air.

00:55:33.480 --> 00:55:34.850
Man, this is fine.

00:55:34.850 --> 00:55:37.380
This is the life.

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:40.710
Glandy Keith Colaco and
Fat Perce Fishwaller

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:44.010
step out of the shadows
of the quadrangle.

00:55:44.010 --> 00:55:45.590
They are giggling.

00:55:45.590 --> 00:55:46.532
He he.

00:55:50.150 --> 00:55:54.520
Masha Miovic looks puzzled
as they block our path.

00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:58.040
'Hoo hoo,' Fat Perce
says, 'Masha, hoo hoo,

00:55:58.040 --> 00:55:59.905
some date you got there.'

00:55:59.905 --> 00:56:02.740
And I, 'Shut up you.'

00:56:02.740 --> 00:56:06.520
Whereupon Glandy Keith, 'You
want to know how he got his war

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:08.350
wound, Masha?'

00:56:08.350 --> 00:56:10.848
And Fat Perce, 'Hee hee hee.'

00:56:10.848 --> 00:56:13.060
Masha says, 'Don't be crude.

00:56:13.060 --> 00:56:15.520
He got it in a
sporting accident.'

00:56:15.520 --> 00:56:19.030
Fat Perce and Glandy Keith are
almost falling over with mirth.

00:56:19.030 --> 00:56:21.285
And then Fishwala reveals all.

00:56:21.285 --> 00:56:24.120
'Zagallo pulled his
hair out in class.

00:56:24.120 --> 00:56:25.290
Hee hoo ha.'

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:27.790
And Keith, 'Snot-nose
is a baldy.'

00:56:27.790 --> 00:56:30.640
And both together, 'the
Sniffer's got a map face.'

00:56:30.640 --> 00:56:35.080
There is puzzlement on
Masha Miovic's face--

00:56:35.080 --> 00:56:39.760
and something more, some budding
spirit of sexual mischief.

00:56:39.760 --> 00:56:43.240
'Saleem, they are being
so rude about you.'

00:56:43.240 --> 00:56:46.210
'Yes,' I say, 'ignore them.'

00:56:46.210 --> 00:56:48.010
I try to edge her away.

00:56:48.010 --> 00:56:49.840
But she goes on.

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:53.670
'You aren't going to let
them get away with it?'

00:56:53.670 --> 00:56:57.240
There are beads of
excitement on her upper lip.

00:56:57.240 --> 00:56:59.670
Her tongue is in the
corner of her mouth.

00:56:59.670 --> 00:57:03.720
The eyes of Masha Miovic say,
what are you, a man or a mouse?

00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:06.210
And under the spell of the
champion breast-stroker,

00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:08.370
something else
floats into my head.

00:57:08.370 --> 00:57:11.250
And now I am rushing
at Colaco and Fishwala.

00:57:11.250 --> 00:57:12.900
While they are
distracted by giggles,

00:57:12.900 --> 00:57:15.690
my knee drives into
Glandy's groin.

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:17.850
Before he's dropped,
a similar genuflection

00:57:17.850 --> 00:57:19.890
has laid Fat Perce low.

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:21.510
I turn to my mistress.

00:57:21.510 --> 00:57:24.180
She applauds softly.

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:26.580
'Hey man, pretty good.'

00:57:26.580 --> 00:57:29.100
But now my moment has
passed and Fat Perce

00:57:29.100 --> 00:57:30.470
is picking himself up.

00:57:30.470 --> 00:57:32.870
And Glandy Keith is
moving towards me.

00:57:32.870 --> 00:57:36.610
Abandoning all pretense
of manhood I turn and run.

00:57:36.610 --> 00:57:38.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:38.960 --> 00:57:40.490
And the two bullies
are after me.

00:57:40.490 --> 00:57:42.680
And behind them comes
Masha Miovic calling,

00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:44.900
'where are you
running, little hero?'

00:57:44.900 --> 00:57:46.350
But there's no time for her now.

00:57:46.350 --> 00:57:50.950
Mustn't let them--
mustn't let them get me.

00:57:50.950 --> 00:57:53.740
Into the nearest classroom,
try and shut the door.

00:57:53.740 --> 00:57:55.536
But Fat Perce's
foot is in the way.

00:57:55.536 --> 00:57:57.160
And now the two of
them are inside too.

00:57:57.160 --> 00:57:58.330
And I dash at the door.

00:57:58.330 --> 00:58:01.120
I grab it with my right hand,
trying to force it open.

00:58:01.120 --> 00:58:02.139
Get out if you can.

00:58:02.139 --> 00:58:03.430
They are pushing the door shut.

00:58:03.430 --> 00:58:05.500
But I'm pulling with
the strength of my fear.

00:58:05.500 --> 00:58:07.120
I have it open a few inches.

00:58:07.120 --> 00:58:08.700
My hand curls around it.

00:58:08.700 --> 00:58:12.100
And now Fat Perce slams all
his weight against the door

00:58:12.100 --> 00:58:15.340
and it shuts too fast for me
to get my hand out of the way

00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:16.578
and it's shut.

00:58:16.578 --> 00:58:19.720
A thud.

00:58:19.720 --> 00:58:23.320
And outside, Masha Miovic
arrives and looks down

00:58:23.320 --> 00:58:25.960
at the floor and
sees the top third

00:58:25.960 --> 00:58:30.300
of my middle finger lying like a
lump of well-chewed bubble gum.

00:58:33.014 --> 00:58:34.680
This was the point
at which she fainted.

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:41.820
No pain.

00:58:41.820 --> 00:58:44.820
Everything very far away.

00:58:44.820 --> 00:58:49.950
Fat Perce and Glandy Keith
fleeing to get help or to hide.

00:58:49.950 --> 00:58:53.020
I look at my hand
out of curiosity.

00:58:53.020 --> 00:58:55.840
My finger has become a fountain.

00:58:55.840 --> 00:59:00.430
Red liquid spurts out to
the rhythm of my heartbeat.

00:59:00.430 --> 00:59:03.996
Never knew a finger
held so much blood.

00:59:03.996 --> 00:59:05.300
Pretty.

00:59:05.300 --> 00:59:06.690
Now here's nurse.

00:59:06.690 --> 00:59:09.289
'Don't worry nurse,
only a scratch.'

00:59:09.289 --> 00:59:10.580
'Your parents are being phoned.

00:59:10.580 --> 00:59:13.970
Mr. Crusoe is getting
his car keys.'

00:59:13.970 --> 00:59:19.690
Nurse is putting a great wad
of cotton wool over the stump.

00:59:19.690 --> 00:59:22.950
It's filling up
like red candyfloss.

00:59:22.950 --> 00:59:24.661
And now Crusoe.

00:59:24.661 --> 00:59:25.660
'Get in the car, Saleem.

00:59:25.660 --> 00:59:27.070
Your mother is going
straight to the hospital.'

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:28.550
'Yes, sir.' 'And the bit?

00:59:28.550 --> 00:59:32.710
Has anybody got the bit?'
'Yes, Headmaster, here it is.'

00:59:32.710 --> 00:59:33.460
'Thank you, nurse.

00:59:33.460 --> 00:59:34.199
Probably no use.

00:59:34.199 --> 00:59:34.990
But you never know.

00:59:34.990 --> 00:59:36.480
Hold this while
I drive, Saleem.'

00:59:39.760 --> 00:59:43.570
And holding up my
severed fingertip

00:59:43.570 --> 00:59:47.620
in my unmutilated left hand,
I am driven to the hospital

00:59:47.620 --> 00:59:50.650
through the echoing
streets of night.

00:59:50.650 --> 00:59:53.110
At the hospital,
white wall stretches.

00:59:53.110 --> 00:59:54.370
Everyone talking at once.

00:59:54.370 --> 00:59:57.340
Words pour around me like
fountains. 'O God preserve us,

00:59:57.340 --> 01:00:00.070
my little piece of the moon,
what have they done to you?'

01:00:00.070 --> 01:00:02.800
To which old Crusoe,
'Heh heh, Mrs. Sinai,

01:00:02.800 --> 01:00:05.680
accidents will happen.

01:00:05.680 --> 01:00:06.880
Boys will be boys.'

01:00:06.880 --> 01:00:08.260
But my mother, enraged.

01:00:08.260 --> 01:00:11.760
'What kind of
school, Mr. Carusoe?'

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:14.500
I am here with my son's finger
in pieces and you tell me.

01:00:14.500 --> 01:00:15.830
Not good enough.

01:00:15.830 --> 01:00:18.400
No, sir.' And now,
while Crusoe--

01:00:18.400 --> 01:00:23.050
'Actually the name is
like Robinson, you know.'

01:00:23.050 --> 01:00:24.970
The doctor is approaching
and the question

01:00:24.970 --> 01:00:29.930
is being asked, whose answer
will change the world.

01:00:29.930 --> 01:00:32.770
'Mrs. Sinai, your
blood group please?

01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:35.970
The boy has lost blood,
transfusion may be necessary.'

01:00:35.970 --> 01:00:39.416
And Amina, I am
A, my husband, O.

01:00:39.416 --> 01:00:42.190
And now she is
crying, breaking down.

01:00:42.190 --> 01:00:46.420
Still the doctor, 'In that case,
are you aware of your son's?'

01:00:46.420 --> 01:00:49.720
But she, the doctor's daughter,
must admit that she cannot

01:00:49.720 --> 01:00:52.986
answer the question,
Alpha or Omega?

01:00:52.986 --> 01:00:54.610
'Well, in that case,
a very quick test,

01:00:54.610 --> 01:00:56.080
but on the subject of rhesus?'

01:00:56.080 --> 01:00:58.615
My mother, through her
tears, both my husband and I

01:00:58.615 --> 01:00:59.900
are rhesus positive.

01:00:59.900 --> 01:01:02.710
And the doctor, 'Well,
good, that at least.'

01:01:02.710 --> 01:01:06.274
But when I am on the operating
table, 'Just sit there, son.

01:01:06.274 --> 01:01:07.690
I'll give you a
local anesthetic.'

01:01:07.690 --> 01:01:08.860
'No madam, he's in shock.

01:01:08.860 --> 01:01:11.260
Total anesthesia would be
impossible.' 'All right, son,

01:01:11.260 --> 01:01:12.627
just hold your finger up.

01:01:12.627 --> 01:01:13.210
Keep it still.

01:01:13.210 --> 01:01:13.876
Help him, nurse.

01:01:13.876 --> 01:01:16.120
It will be over in a jiffy.'

01:01:16.120 --> 01:01:19.090
While the surgeon is
sewing up the stump

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:21.100
and performing the
miracle of transplanting

01:01:21.100 --> 01:01:23.560
the roots of the
nail, all of a sudden

01:01:23.560 --> 01:01:25.530
there's a fluster
in the background

01:01:25.530 --> 01:01:27.280
a million miles away.

01:01:27.280 --> 01:01:30.960
And 'Have you got a
second, Mrs. Sinai?'

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:33.340
And I can't hear properly.

01:01:33.340 --> 01:01:37.550
Words float across
the infinite distance.

01:01:37.550 --> 01:01:40.270
'Mrs. Sinai, are you sure?

01:01:40.270 --> 01:01:42.960
O and A?

01:01:42.960 --> 01:01:45.268
A and O?

01:01:45.268 --> 01:01:48.230
Rhesus positive, both of you?

01:01:48.230 --> 01:01:51.697
Heterozygous or homozygous?'

01:01:51.697 --> 01:01:53.030
'No, there must be some mistake.

01:01:53.030 --> 01:01:56.960
How could--' 'I'm sorry,
it's absolutely clear.'

01:01:56.960 --> 01:02:00.160
'Neither A nor--' 'Excuse
me, Madam, but he is your--

01:02:03.180 --> 01:02:05.590
not adopted or--'

01:02:05.590 --> 01:02:07.290
The hospital nurse
interposes herself

01:02:07.290 --> 01:02:10.080
between me and the
miles-away chatter.

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:12.567
But it's no good because
now my mother is shrieking.

01:02:12.567 --> 01:02:14.400
'But of course, you
must believe me, Doctor.

01:02:14.400 --> 01:02:15.810
My god, of course,
he's our son.'

01:02:18.700 --> 01:02:25.160
'Neither A nor O. And the rhesus
factor impossibly negative.

01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:27.830
And zygosity offers no clues.

01:02:27.830 --> 01:02:32.150
And present in the blood,
rare Kell antibodies.'

01:02:32.150 --> 01:02:36.870
And my mother crying,
crying, crying, crying.

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:38.910
'I don't understand.

01:02:38.910 --> 01:02:42.390
A doctor's daughter and
I don't understand.'

01:02:42.390 --> 01:02:45.346
Have Alpha and
Omega unmasked me?

01:02:45.346 --> 01:02:50.000
Is rhesus pointing its
unanswerable finger?

01:02:50.000 --> 01:02:54.650
And will Mary Pereira
be obliged to confess?

01:02:54.650 --> 01:02:58.370
I wake up in a cool, white,
Venetian-blinded room

01:02:58.370 --> 01:03:00.530
with All-India
Radio for company.

01:03:00.530 --> 01:03:05.680
Tony Brent is singing
'Red Sails in The Sunset.'

01:03:05.680 --> 01:03:09.760
My father, Ahmed Sinai,
his face ravaged by whiskey

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:11.950
and now by something
worse stands

01:03:11.950 --> 01:03:14.310
beside the Venetian blind.

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:18.010
Amina speaking in whispers.

01:03:18.010 --> 01:03:23.550
Again, I hear snatches across
the million miles of distance.

01:03:23.550 --> 01:03:25.050
'Please, I beg you.

01:03:25.050 --> 01:03:26.690
No, what are you saying?

01:03:26.690 --> 01:03:28.040
Of course it was.

01:03:28.040 --> 01:03:30.450
Of course you are the--

01:03:30.450 --> 01:03:33.451
how could you think I would--
who could it have been?

01:03:33.451 --> 01:03:35.199
Oh God, don't just
stand and look.

01:03:35.199 --> 01:03:36.740
I swear, I swear on
my mother's head.

01:03:36.740 --> 01:03:38.670
Now she is--'

01:03:38.670 --> 01:03:40.770
A new song from Tony
Brent, whose repertoire

01:03:40.770 --> 01:03:44.350
today is uncanny.

01:03:44.350 --> 01:03:48.100
'How Much is That
Doggie in the Window?'

01:03:48.100 --> 01:03:52.880
hangs in the air,
floating on radio waves.

01:03:52.880 --> 01:03:57.320
My father advances on
me, towers over me.

01:03:57.320 --> 01:04:00.602
I've never seen him
look like this before.

01:04:00.602 --> 01:04:04.080
'Abba.' And he, 'I
should have known.

01:04:04.080 --> 01:04:04.590
Just look.

01:04:04.590 --> 01:04:05.200
Where am I?

01:04:05.200 --> 01:04:08.102
In that face, that nose,
I should have known.'

01:04:08.102 --> 01:04:09.810
He turns on his heel
and leaves the room.

01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:12.467
My mother follows him, too
distraught to whisper now.

01:04:12.467 --> 01:04:14.550
'No, I won't let you believe
such things about me.

01:04:14.550 --> 01:04:15.330
I'll kill myself.

01:04:15.330 --> 01:04:20.680
I'll--' And the door
swings shut behind them.

01:04:20.680 --> 01:04:28.370
There is a noise outside
like a clap or a slap.

01:04:28.370 --> 01:04:30.590
Most of what
matters in your life

01:04:30.590 --> 01:04:33.660
takes place in your absence.

01:04:33.660 --> 01:04:39.260
Tony Brent begins crooning his
latest hit into my good ear

01:04:39.260 --> 01:04:43.940
and assures me, melodiously,
that 'The Clouds Will Soon Roll

01:04:43.940 --> 01:04:46.560
By'."

01:04:46.560 --> 01:04:49.400
That will do for him.

01:04:49.400 --> 01:04:50.155
Thanks.

01:04:50.155 --> 01:04:54.115
[APPLAUSE]

01:05:15.534 --> 01:05:17.700
If you can stand another
one, I'll read another one.

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:24.190
I read those faster
than I usually do, so.

01:05:24.190 --> 01:05:25.390
This is a short story.

01:05:25.390 --> 01:05:28.060
So I thought at least I could
read one small complete piece

01:05:28.060 --> 01:05:29.186
of work.

01:05:29.186 --> 01:05:33.370
This is a more recent short
story, not about India.

01:05:33.370 --> 01:05:36.510
This is a short story
with a very long title.

01:05:36.510 --> 01:05:39.300
It's called "Christopher
Columbus and Queen Isabella

01:05:39.300 --> 01:05:45.280
of Spain Consummate Their
Relationship, Santa Fe,

01:05:45.280 --> 01:05:52.920
January 1492."

01:05:52.920 --> 01:05:58.740
"Columbus, a foreigner, follows
Queen Isabella for an eternity

01:05:58.740 --> 01:06:02.770
without entirely giving up hope.

01:06:02.770 --> 01:06:07.290
In what characteristic
postures, proud yet supplicant,

01:06:07.290 --> 01:06:11.550
the head held high, but the knee
bent, fawning, yet fearless,

01:06:11.550 --> 01:06:13.830
possessed of a certain
saucy vulgarity,

01:06:13.830 --> 01:06:18.210
he gets away with it by virtue
of his confidence man's charm.

01:06:18.210 --> 01:06:22.470
However, as time passes,
the ingratiating aspects

01:06:22.470 --> 01:06:25.110
of his stance are emphasized.

01:06:25.110 --> 01:06:31.970
The sea dog raffishness wears
a little thin, as do his shoes.

01:06:31.970 --> 01:06:35.490
His hope, it is of what?

01:06:35.490 --> 01:06:37.050
Obvious answers first.

01:06:37.050 --> 01:06:39.810
He hopes for preferment.

01:06:39.810 --> 01:06:43.590
He wants to tie the queen's
favor to his helmet,

01:06:43.590 --> 01:06:45.780
like a knight in a romance.

01:06:45.780 --> 01:06:48.550
He owns no helmet.

01:06:48.550 --> 01:06:53.410
He has hopes of cash and of
three tall ships, Niña, Pinta,

01:06:53.410 --> 01:07:00.070
Santa Maria, of, in 1492,
sailing across the ocean blue.

01:07:00.070 --> 01:07:02.680
But on his first
arrival at court,

01:07:02.680 --> 01:07:05.950
when the Queen herself
asked him what he desired,

01:07:05.950 --> 01:07:09.990
he bowed over her olive
hand, and with his lips

01:07:09.990 --> 01:07:13.570
a millimeter away from the
great ring of her power,

01:07:13.570 --> 01:07:21.420
he murmured a single
dangerous word, consummation.

01:07:21.420 --> 01:07:27.990
'These unspeakable foreigners,
the nerve, consummation indeed,

01:07:27.990 --> 01:07:30.270
and then following
in her footsteps

01:07:30.270 --> 01:07:33.510
month after month as
if he stood a chance.

01:07:33.510 --> 01:07:36.900
His coarse epistles,
his tuneless serenades

01:07:36.900 --> 01:07:40.350
beneath her casement windows,
obliging her to have them

01:07:40.350 --> 01:07:43.530
closed, shutting out
the cooling breeze.

01:07:43.530 --> 01:07:47.250
She had better things to do, a
world to conquer and so forth.

01:07:47.250 --> 01:07:48.450
Who did he think he was?'

01:07:51.190 --> 01:07:55.040
Foreigners can be dogged--

01:07:55.040 --> 01:07:57.890
and also on a count of
language difficulties,

01:07:57.890 --> 01:07:59.260
fail to take a hint.

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:06.720
Then, again, let us not forget,
it is considered de rigueur

01:08:06.720 --> 01:08:09.390
to keep a few foreigners around.

01:08:09.390 --> 01:08:13.900
They lend the place a
certain cosmopolitan tone.

01:08:13.900 --> 01:08:16.540
They are often poor
and consequently

01:08:16.540 --> 01:08:20.920
willing to perform diverse
necessary, but dirty jobs.

01:08:20.920 --> 01:08:24.370
They are, moreover, a
warning against complacency.

01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:27.220
Their existence in
our midst reminding us

01:08:27.220 --> 01:08:30.819
that there are quarters in
which, hard as it is to accept,

01:08:30.819 --> 01:08:35.300
we ourselves would be
considered foreigners to.

01:08:35.300 --> 01:08:38.939
'But to speak so to the Queen.'

01:08:38.939 --> 01:08:42.930
Foreigners forget their
place, having left it behind.

01:08:45.450 --> 01:08:50.250
Given time, they begin to think
of themselves as our equals.

01:08:50.250 --> 01:08:52.850
It is an unavoidable hazard.

01:08:52.850 --> 01:08:55.100
They introduce into
our austerities

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:58.649
their Italianate blandishments.

01:08:58.649 --> 01:09:01.529
Nothing for it, turn a deaf
ear, look the other way.

01:09:01.529 --> 01:09:07.319
They rarely mean real harm and
go too far only infrequently.

01:09:07.319 --> 01:09:12.200
The Queen, be assured,
can look after itself.

01:09:12.200 --> 01:09:14.620
Columbus, at Isabella's
court, is quickly

01:09:14.620 --> 01:09:18.250
burdened with the
reputation of a crazy man.

01:09:18.250 --> 01:09:20.649
His clothes are
excessively colorful.

01:09:20.649 --> 01:09:24.069
And he drinks also to excess.

01:09:24.069 --> 01:09:25.960
When Isabella wins
a military victory,

01:09:25.960 --> 01:09:28.930
she celebrates it
with 11 days of Psalms

01:09:28.930 --> 01:09:31.960
and the sonorous
severities of priests.

01:09:31.960 --> 01:09:34.660
Columbus crashes about
outside the cathedral

01:09:34.660 --> 01:09:37.240
waving a wine skin.

01:09:37.240 --> 01:09:38.590
He is a one man debauch.

01:09:41.279 --> 01:09:45.870
'See, him, that drunkard, with
his huge shaggy head filled

01:09:45.870 --> 01:09:50.399
with nonsenses, a fool with
a glittering eye dreaming

01:09:50.399 --> 01:09:54.080
of a golden paradise beyond
the Western edge of things.'

01:09:57.490 --> 01:09:58.510
Consummation.

01:10:01.270 --> 01:10:05.100
The queen plays with Columbus.

01:10:05.100 --> 01:10:07.710
She promises him everything
he wants at luncheon

01:10:07.710 --> 01:10:10.050
and cuts him dead later
the same afternoon,

01:10:10.050 --> 01:10:13.540
looking through him
as if he were a veil.

01:10:13.540 --> 01:10:16.840
On his Saint's day, she summons
him into her inmost boudoir,

01:10:16.840 --> 01:10:19.750
dismisses her girls, permits
him to braid her hair,

01:10:19.750 --> 01:10:22.660
and for a moment, to
fondle her breasts.

01:10:22.660 --> 01:10:24.280
Then she summons her guards.

01:10:24.280 --> 01:10:27.760
For 40 days she banishes him
to the stables and piggeries.

01:10:27.760 --> 01:10:30.550
He sits forlorn on
horse-munched hay

01:10:30.550 --> 01:10:33.880
while his thoughts run
on distant fabled gold.

01:10:33.880 --> 01:10:36.370
He dreams of the
Queen's perfumes

01:10:36.370 --> 01:10:40.220
but awakes gagging in a pigsty.

01:10:40.220 --> 01:10:43.930
Toying with Columbus
pleases the Queen.

01:10:43.930 --> 01:10:45.330
At pleasing the
Queen, he reminds

01:10:45.330 --> 01:10:48.330
himself, may help him
to achieve his purposes.

01:10:48.330 --> 01:10:50.700
Pigs rootle by his feet.

01:10:50.700 --> 01:10:52.320
He grits his teeth.

01:10:52.320 --> 01:10:56.500
Pleasing the Queen is good.

01:10:56.500 --> 01:11:03.150
Does she torment him merely for
sport or because he is foreign

01:11:03.150 --> 01:11:06.280
and she is unused to
his ways and meanings

01:11:06.280 --> 01:11:09.440
or because her ring finger--

01:11:09.440 --> 01:11:12.380
still hot with the memory
of his lips, his breath--

01:11:12.380 --> 01:11:16.300
has been, how you say, touched.

01:11:16.300 --> 01:11:18.850
Tentacles of warmth spread
backwards from her finger

01:11:18.850 --> 01:11:20.220
towards her heart.

01:11:20.220 --> 01:11:23.500
A turbulence has been aroused.

01:11:23.500 --> 01:11:27.460
Or because she is torn between
the possibility of embracing

01:11:27.460 --> 01:11:30.490
his scheme with a lover's
abandon and the more

01:11:30.490 --> 01:11:33.760
conventional and
differently, maliciously,

01:11:33.760 --> 01:11:37.570
pleasurable option of destroying
him by laughing finally

01:11:37.570 --> 01:11:42.280
after much foreplay in his
foolish, supplicant face.

01:11:42.280 --> 01:11:45.580
Columbus consoles himself
with possibilities.

01:11:45.580 --> 01:11:49.540
Not all possibilities
are consoling, however.

01:11:49.540 --> 01:11:51.730
She is an absolute monarch.

01:11:51.730 --> 01:11:55.870
Her husband is an
absolute zero, a blank.

01:11:55.870 --> 01:11:57.520
A blank couldn't be colder.

01:11:57.520 --> 01:11:59.770
We will not speak
further of him.

01:11:59.770 --> 01:12:04.320
She is a woman whose
ring is often kissed.

01:12:04.320 --> 01:12:06.060
It means nothing to her.

01:12:06.060 --> 01:12:08.160
She is no stranger
to blandishments.

01:12:08.160 --> 01:12:10.200
She resists them effortlessly.

01:12:10.200 --> 01:12:12.930
She is a tyrant who numbers
among her possessions

01:12:12.930 --> 01:12:17.520
a private menagerie of 419
fools, some grotesquely

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:20.800
malformed others, as
beauteous as the dawn.

01:12:20.800 --> 01:12:25.310
He, Columbus, is
merely her 420th idiot.

01:12:25.310 --> 01:12:28.290
He is her clown,
her performing flea.

01:12:28.290 --> 01:12:32.280
This too is a
plausible scenario.

01:12:32.280 --> 01:12:36.560
Either she understands
him, his dream

01:12:36.560 --> 01:12:38.870
of a world beyond
the world's end

01:12:38.870 --> 01:12:41.990
so profoundly that
she's spooked by it,

01:12:41.990 --> 01:12:45.730
she turns first
towards it, then away,

01:12:45.730 --> 01:12:53.960
or she doesn't understand him
at all nor cares to understand.

01:12:53.960 --> 01:12:56.320
Take your pick.

01:12:56.320 --> 01:12:59.890
What's certain is that he
doesn't understand her.

01:12:59.890 --> 01:13:02.200
Only the facts are plain.

01:13:02.200 --> 01:13:05.560
She is Isabella,
all-conquering queen.

01:13:05.560 --> 01:13:09.990
He is her invisible, though
raucous, multicolored,

01:13:09.990 --> 01:13:11.550
winebibbing man.

01:13:14.520 --> 01:13:15.420
Consummation.

01:13:18.050 --> 01:13:21.250
The sexual appetites
of the male decline.

01:13:21.250 --> 01:13:24.070
Those of the female continue
with the advancing years

01:13:24.070 --> 01:13:25.720
to grow.

01:13:25.720 --> 01:13:27.940
Isabella is Columbus' last hope.

01:13:27.940 --> 01:13:30.790
He is running out of
possible patrons, sales talk,

01:13:30.790 --> 01:13:34.570
flirtatiousness, hair, steam.

01:13:34.570 --> 01:13:36.430
Time drags by.

01:13:36.430 --> 01:13:39.490
Isabella gallops around,
winning battles, expelling Moors

01:13:39.490 --> 01:13:41.500
from their strongholds,
her appetites

01:13:41.500 --> 01:13:43.850
expanding by the week.

01:13:43.850 --> 01:13:45.470
The more of the
land she swallows,

01:13:45.470 --> 01:13:48.300
the hungrier she gets.

01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:51.710
The more warriors she engulfs,
the hungrier she gets.

01:13:51.710 --> 01:13:55.970
Columbus, aware of the
slow, shriveling inside him,

01:13:55.970 --> 01:13:57.920
scolds himself.

01:13:57.920 --> 01:13:59.860
He should see
things as they are.

01:13:59.860 --> 01:14:01.540
He should come to his senses.

01:14:01.540 --> 01:14:03.910
What chance does he have here?

01:14:03.910 --> 01:14:07.690
Some days, she makes
him clean the latrines.

01:14:07.690 --> 01:14:09.820
On other days, he is
on body washing duty.

01:14:09.820 --> 01:14:12.960
And after a battle, the
bodies are not clean.

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:15.720
Soldiers go into war
wearing man-sized nappies

01:14:15.720 --> 01:14:18.180
under their armor
because the fear of death

01:14:18.180 --> 01:14:22.520
will open the bowels,
will do it every time.

01:14:22.520 --> 01:14:25.270
Columbus was not cut out
for this sort of work.

01:14:25.270 --> 01:14:26.570
It is getting him nowhere.

01:14:26.570 --> 01:14:31.130
He tells himself to leave
Isabella once and for all.

01:14:31.130 --> 01:14:32.620
But there are problems--

01:14:32.620 --> 01:14:35.665
his advancing years,
the patron shortage.

01:14:39.190 --> 01:14:43.960
Once he decamps, will have
to forget the Western voyage.

01:14:43.960 --> 01:14:45.580
The body of
philosophical opinion

01:14:45.580 --> 01:14:50.270
which avers that life is absurd
has never appealed to him.

01:14:50.270 --> 01:14:54.510
He is a man of action,
revealing himself by deeds.

01:14:54.510 --> 01:14:56.760
But without the
possibility of the voyage,

01:14:56.760 --> 01:15:00.760
he will be obliged to accept
the meaninglessness of life.

01:15:00.760 --> 01:15:03.670
This too would be a defeat.

01:15:03.670 --> 01:15:06.220
Invisible in hot
tropical colors,

01:15:06.220 --> 01:15:09.550
unrequited, he remains
dogging her footsteps,

01:15:09.550 --> 01:15:13.410
hoping for the
ecstasy of her glance.

01:15:13.410 --> 01:15:15.450
The search for
money and patronage,

01:15:15.450 --> 01:15:18.630
Columbus says, is not so
different from the quest

01:15:18.630 --> 01:15:19.130
for love.

01:15:21.640 --> 01:15:24.170
'She is omnipotent.

01:15:24.170 --> 01:15:26.620
Castles fall at her feet.

01:15:26.620 --> 01:15:29.260
The Jews are being expelled.

01:15:29.260 --> 01:15:32.290
The Moors prepare
their last surrender.

01:15:32.290 --> 01:15:37.300
The Queen is at Granada,
riding at her Army's head.'

01:15:37.300 --> 01:15:39.340
She overwhelms.

01:15:39.340 --> 01:15:43.000
Nothing she has wanted
has ever been refused.

01:15:43.000 --> 01:15:46.030
All her dreams are prophecies.

01:15:46.030 --> 01:15:48.550
Acting upon information
received while sleeping,

01:15:48.550 --> 01:15:51.300
she draws up her
invincible battle plans,

01:15:51.300 --> 01:15:53.620
foils the conspiracies
of assassins,

01:15:53.620 --> 01:15:55.690
learns of the infidelities
and corruptions

01:15:55.690 --> 01:15:58.540
for which she blackmails
both her loyalists

01:15:58.540 --> 01:16:02.860
to ensure their support and
her opponents to ensure theirs.

01:16:02.860 --> 01:16:05.260
The dreams help her
forecast the weather,

01:16:05.260 --> 01:16:10.430
negotiate treaties, and
invest shrewdly in trade.

01:16:10.430 --> 01:16:14.210
'She eats like a horse
and never gains an ounce.

01:16:14.210 --> 01:16:16.940
The earth adores her footfall.

01:16:16.940 --> 01:16:21.070
Its shadows flee before
the brilliance of her eyes.

01:16:21.070 --> 01:16:26.190
Her face is a lush peninsula
set in a sea of hair.

01:16:26.190 --> 01:16:30.810
Her treasure chests
are inexhaustible.

01:16:30.810 --> 01:16:37.140
Her ears a soft question marks,
suggesting some uncertainty.

01:16:37.140 --> 01:16:39.690
Her legs--' her legs
are not so great.

01:16:42.540 --> 01:16:45.660
She is full of discontents.

01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:47.910
No conquest satisfies her.

01:16:47.910 --> 01:16:51.000
No peak of ecstasy
is high enough.

01:16:51.000 --> 01:16:54.000
See, there at the
gates of the Alhambra

01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:57.370
is Boabdil the Unlucky,
the last sultan

01:16:57.370 --> 01:17:01.080
of the last redoubt of all
the centuries of Arab Spain.

01:17:01.080 --> 01:17:04.860
Behold, now, at
this very instant,

01:17:04.860 --> 01:17:09.212
he surrenders the keys to
the citadel into her grasp.

01:17:09.212 --> 01:17:10.900
There.

01:17:10.900 --> 01:17:14.410
And as the weight of the
keys falls from his hand

01:17:14.410 --> 01:17:19.860
into hers, she, she yawns.

01:17:22.710 --> 01:17:25.500
Columbus gives up hope.

01:17:25.500 --> 01:17:29.100
While Isabella is entering the
Alhambra in listless triumph,

01:17:29.100 --> 01:17:31.380
he is saddling his mule.

01:17:31.380 --> 01:17:33.770
While she dawdles in the
Courtyard of the Lions,

01:17:33.770 --> 01:17:37.080
he departs in a frenzy
of whips, elbows, hooves,

01:17:37.080 --> 01:17:40.680
all rapidly obscured
by a dust cloud.

01:17:40.680 --> 01:17:42.840
Invisibility claims him.

01:17:42.840 --> 01:17:45.320
He surrenders to its will.

01:17:45.320 --> 01:17:50.520
Knowing he is abandoning
his destiny, he abandons it.

01:17:50.520 --> 01:17:53.850
He rides away from Queen
Isabella in hopeless fury,

01:17:53.850 --> 01:17:55.380
rides day and night.

01:17:55.380 --> 01:17:57.650
And when his mule
dies under him,

01:17:57.650 --> 01:17:59.520
he shoulders his
ridiculous gypsy

01:17:59.520 --> 01:18:03.600
patchwork bags, their rowdy
colors muted now by dirt

01:18:03.600 --> 01:18:05.320
and walks.

01:18:05.320 --> 01:18:10.180
Around him stretches the lush
plain her armies have subdued.

01:18:10.180 --> 01:18:12.340
Columbus sees none of it--

01:18:12.340 --> 01:18:15.280
not the land's fertility
nor the sudden barrenness

01:18:15.280 --> 01:18:17.140
of the vanquished
castles looking down

01:18:17.140 --> 01:18:18.670
from their pinnacles.

01:18:18.670 --> 01:18:21.370
The ghosts of
defeated civilizations

01:18:21.370 --> 01:18:26.260
flow unnoticed down the rivers,
Guadal-this and Guadal-that,

01:18:26.260 --> 01:18:30.280
their names retaining an
echo of the annihilated past.

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:33.360
Overhead, the arabesque
wheelings of the patient

01:18:33.360 --> 01:18:35.020
buzzards.

01:18:35.020 --> 01:18:37.960
Jews pass Columbus
in long columns.

01:18:37.960 --> 01:18:41.260
But the tragedy of their
expulsion makes no mark on him.

01:18:41.260 --> 01:18:43.570
Somebody tries to sell
him a Toledo sword.

01:18:43.570 --> 01:18:45.860
He waves the man away.

01:18:45.860 --> 01:18:48.500
Having lost his
own dream of ships,

01:18:48.500 --> 01:18:52.910
Columbus leaves the Jews to the
ships of their exile waiting

01:18:52.910 --> 01:18:55.010
in the harbor of Cádiz.

01:18:55.010 --> 01:18:58.520
Exhaustion strips
him of his senses.

01:18:58.520 --> 01:19:00.830
This Old World is too old.

01:19:00.830 --> 01:19:04.770
And the New World
is an unfound land.

01:19:04.770 --> 01:19:07.950
The loss of money and
patronage, Columbus says,

01:19:07.950 --> 01:19:11.210
is as bitter as unrequited love.

01:19:11.210 --> 01:19:14.810
He walks beyond fatigue,
beyond the limits of endurance

01:19:14.810 --> 01:19:16.730
and the frontiers of self.

01:19:16.730 --> 01:19:19.550
And somewhere along this
path, he loses his balance,

01:19:19.550 --> 01:19:21.800
falls off the edge
of his sanity.

01:19:21.800 --> 01:19:24.240
And out here, beyond
his mind's rim,

01:19:24.240 --> 01:19:29.360
he sees, for the first and only
time in his life, a vision.

01:19:29.360 --> 01:19:32.120
It is the dream of a dream.

01:19:32.120 --> 01:19:35.390
He dreams of Isabella
languidly exploring

01:19:35.390 --> 01:19:38.570
the Alhambra, the great jewel
she has seized from Boabdil

01:19:38.570 --> 01:19:40.640
last of the Nasrids.

01:19:40.640 --> 01:19:43.670
She is staring into a
large stone bowl held aloft

01:19:43.670 --> 01:19:45.590
by stony lions.

01:19:45.590 --> 01:19:48.490
The bowl is filled with blood.

01:19:48.490 --> 01:19:53.610
And in it she sees,
Columbus dreams her seeing,

01:19:53.610 --> 01:19:56.370
a vision of her own.

01:19:56.370 --> 01:19:59.480
The bowl shows her
that everything,

01:19:59.480 --> 01:20:02.700
all the known world is now hers.

01:20:02.700 --> 01:20:07.010
Everyone in it is in her hands
to do with as she pleases.

01:20:07.010 --> 01:20:10.960
And when she understands this,
Columbus dreams, the blood

01:20:10.960 --> 01:20:15.950
at once congeals, becoming a
thick and verminous sludge.

01:20:15.950 --> 01:20:19.280
Whereupon the Isabella of
Columbus' weary but also

01:20:19.280 --> 01:20:23.300
vengeful imaginings is shaken
to her marrow by the realization

01:20:23.300 --> 01:20:25.550
that she will never,
never, never be

01:20:25.550 --> 01:20:29.030
satisfied by the
possession of the known,

01:20:29.030 --> 01:20:35.000
only the unknown, perhaps even
the unknowable can satisfy her.

01:20:35.000 --> 01:20:37.240
And at once she
remembers Columbus.

01:20:37.240 --> 01:20:40.230
He envisions her
remembering him.

01:20:40.230 --> 01:20:44.760
The invisible man who dreams of
entering the invisible world,

01:20:44.760 --> 01:20:48.720
the unknown, perhaps
even unknowable world

01:20:48.720 --> 01:20:52.560
beyond the edge of things,
beyond the stone bowl

01:20:52.560 --> 01:20:57.200
of the everyday, beyond
thick blood of the sea.

01:20:57.200 --> 01:21:00.230
Columbus, in this bitter dream,
makes Isabella see the truth

01:21:00.230 --> 01:21:03.830
that lust makes accept
that her need for him is

01:21:03.830 --> 01:21:06.770
as great as his need for her.

01:21:06.770 --> 01:21:08.390
Yes, she knows it now.

01:21:08.390 --> 01:21:11.900
She must, must, must give him
the money, the ships, anything.

01:21:11.900 --> 01:21:15.500
And he must, must, must
carry her flag and her favor

01:21:15.500 --> 01:21:20.030
beyond the end of the Earth
into exaltation and immortality,

01:21:20.030 --> 01:21:22.790
linking them forever
with bonds far harder

01:21:22.790 --> 01:21:25.730
to dissolve than those
of any mortal love,

01:21:25.730 --> 01:21:30.880
the harsh and deifying
ties of history.

01:21:30.880 --> 01:21:33.380
Consummation.

01:21:33.380 --> 01:21:37.070
Isabella, in Columbus's
savage dream, tears her hair,

01:21:37.070 --> 01:21:40.310
runs from the courtyard of the
Lions, screams for her heralds.

01:21:40.310 --> 01:21:41.000
'Find him!'

01:21:41.000 --> 01:21:42.930
She commands.

01:21:42.930 --> 01:21:46.980
But Columbus, in his
dream, refuses to be found.

01:21:46.980 --> 01:21:50.010
He wraps around himself
the dusty patchwork cloak

01:21:50.010 --> 01:21:51.450
of his invisibility.

01:21:51.450 --> 01:21:54.660
And the heralds gallop
hither and yon in vain.

01:21:54.660 --> 01:21:59.070
Isabella screeches,
beseeches, implores.

01:21:59.070 --> 01:22:00.840
'Bitch, bitch, how
do you like it now?'

01:22:00.840 --> 01:22:02.880
Columbus sneers.

01:22:02.880 --> 01:22:06.000
By absenting himself from
her court by this final

01:22:06.000 --> 01:22:09.000
and suicidal invisibility,
he has denied her her

01:22:09.000 --> 01:22:11.370
heart's desire. 'Serves
her right, bitch.'

01:22:11.370 --> 01:22:12.900
She murdered his
hopes, didn't she?

01:22:12.900 --> 01:22:16.170
Well then, in doing so, she
has laid herself low as well.

01:22:16.170 --> 01:22:17.190
Poetic justice.

01:22:17.190 --> 01:22:19.970
Fair is fair.

01:22:19.970 --> 01:22:21.220
At the dream's end.

01:22:21.220 --> 01:22:24.130
He permits the
messengers to find him.

01:22:24.130 --> 01:22:26.200
Their hoof beats.

01:22:26.200 --> 01:22:29.020
They're waving frantic arms.

01:22:29.020 --> 01:22:32.950
They plead, cajole,
offer bribes.

01:22:32.950 --> 01:22:34.450
But it's too late.

01:22:34.450 --> 01:22:39.130
Only the sweet self lacerating
joy of murdering possibility

01:22:39.130 --> 01:22:40.300
remains.

01:22:40.300 --> 01:22:44.430
He answers the herald, a
shake of the head, 'No.'

01:22:47.230 --> 01:22:50.330
He comes to his senses.

01:22:50.330 --> 01:22:53.390
He is on his knees in the
fertility of the plains waiting

01:22:53.390 --> 01:22:55.530
for death.

01:22:55.530 --> 01:22:58.170
He hears the hoof
beats approaching

01:22:58.170 --> 01:23:01.040
and raises his
eyes, half expecting

01:23:01.040 --> 01:23:03.050
to see the exterminating
angel riding

01:23:03.050 --> 01:23:07.400
towards him like a conqueror,
its black wings, the boredom

01:23:07.400 --> 01:23:09.510
on its face.

01:23:09.510 --> 01:23:11.130
Isabella's heralds surround him.

01:23:11.130 --> 01:23:13.200
They offer him food,
drink, a horse.

01:23:13.200 --> 01:23:15.570
''Good news,' they shout,
'the Queen has summoned you.

01:23:15.570 --> 01:23:19.020
Your voyage, wonderful news,
she saw a vision and it scared

01:23:19.020 --> 01:23:21.140
her.'

01:23:21.140 --> 01:23:24.700
All her dreams are prophecies.

01:23:24.700 --> 01:23:27.400
'She ran from the Courtyard
of the Lions shouting for you,

01:23:27.400 --> 01:23:28.840
the heralds report.

01:23:28.840 --> 01:23:32.290
She will send you beyond the
stone bowl of the known world,

01:23:32.290 --> 01:23:35.200
beyond the thick
blood of the sea.

01:23:35.200 --> 01:23:37.660
She is waiting for
you in Santa Fe.

01:23:37.660 --> 01:23:41.440
You must come at once.'

01:23:41.440 --> 01:23:46.210
He stands up like
a requited lover,

01:23:46.210 --> 01:23:49.280
like a groom on his wedding day.

01:23:49.280 --> 01:23:51.450
He opens his mouth.

01:23:51.450 --> 01:23:56.210
And what almost spills out
is the bitter refusal, no.

01:23:58.860 --> 01:24:01.650
'Yes,' he tells the heralds.

01:24:01.650 --> 01:24:03.870
'Yes, I'll come.'

01:24:03.870 --> 01:24:05.370
Thank you.

01:24:05.370 --> 01:24:08.420
[APPLAUSE]

